12. Why do we restore the character?

          Suppose we try:    A → B → C
          and that branch eventually fails.

          If we leave:
                board[row][col] = '#'
          then that cell would remain unavailable for completely unrelated branches.
          
          That would incorrectly turn: visited for current path
          
          into: visited forever
          
          So: board[row][col] = original;
          
          is not optional.

It is the backtracking invariant:
                    After a recursive branch returns, 
                    the board must be restored to exactly the state
                    it had before the branch was explored.



==========================================================================================================================================
13. Why can't we use a global visited set?

      A common mistake is:         Set<Cell> visited;      
                                  and never removing cells.

        That is wrong because another path must be allowed to reuse the cell.
        
          Example:
          
          Path 1:
                  A → B → C
                      ↓
                    failure        
        Path 2:
                A → D → C
        
        C may be valid in Path 2 even though it was used in Path 1.
        
        Therefore:  visited state = current recursion path

not: all cells ever visited
==========================================================================================================================================
14. Why do we increment index + 1?
            
            Unlike Combination Sum: backtrack(..., i)            
            there is no reuse of the same logical character position in the word.
            
            Once the current cell has matched:  word[index]            
            the next recursion must find:  word[index + 1]            
            So: backtrack(..., index + 1);
            
            This gives another excellent pattern distinction:
            
                          Combination Sum
                              → same candidate reusable
                              → i
                          
                          Word Search
                              → next character required
                              → index + 1
                          
            But remember that the meaning of i and index is completely different.

==========================================================================================================================================
15. Complexity
                  Let:                 
                          R = number of rows
                          C = number of columns
                          L = word.length()
                  
                  We can start a search from each cell:     R×C

                  At the first matched cell, there can be up to 4 directions.                  
                  After moving to a neighboring cell, we cannot immediately return to the previous cell because it is marked visited.
                  So beyond the first step, there are at most 3 new choices per level.
                  
                  Therefore the worst-case time complexity is approximately:   O(R⋅C⋅4⋅3^L−1)
                  which is commonly simplified to: O(RC3^L)

                  The exact bound can be lower depending on the grid and word.
==========================================================================================================================================
6. Space complexity
                The maximum recursion depth is: L
                
                because we match one character per level.
                
                Since we're modifying the grid in place, we don't need a separate visited matrix.                
                Therefore auxiliary space is:   O(L)
                
                excluding the input grid and output.

                This is an important engineering trade-off:
                
                In-place marking :     Space = O(L)
                Separate boolean[][] :  Space = O(RC + L)
                
                The separate matrix may be easier to reason about and is sometimes preferable when mutating caller-owned input is undesirable.
==========================================================================================================================================
17. Production-quality trade-off: mutate input or not?
          
          This is where I want you to start thinking beyond LeetCode.
          
          In an interview:  board[row][col] = '#';          
                            is concise and memory efficient.
          
          In production code, you should ask:          
                            Is the input object owned by this method, or is it shared with other callers?
          
          If the board is shared or immutable-by-contract, modifying it—even temporarily—may violate API expectations.
          
          Then I'd use: boolean[][] visited     instead.
          
          So an L6-level discussion is not:   "In-place marking is better."
          
          It is:
          
                  "In-place marking minimizes auxiliary memory but mutates input temporarily. 
                    A separate visited structure preserves input immutability at the cost of O(RC) additional memory.
                    The right choice depends on API ownership and concurrency expectations."
          
          That's the engineering judgment I want you to practice.
==========================================================================================================================================
18. Stronger pruning:
                  There's an additional optimization you should know.
                  
                  Before DFS, we can count character frequencies in:
                  
                            board
                            word
                  
                  If the board doesn't contain enough occurrences of some required character, return false immediately.
                  
                  For example:
                                word = "AAB"                  
                                but the board contains only one A.
                                Impossible.

                    This is a valid global feasibility pruning.

